\description{
Check, before any grafting, whether the taxa (or missing clades) of \code{missing_taxa_list} can be grafted** on the backbone \code{phy} with the constraints chosen
(target clades, excluded subclades, stem availability, species attributes), and report what is available for each of them: the number of branches, the share of
the available branches that is in the allowed states (if \code{simmap} is used as a constraint), the time window of the divergence ages and the share of it where grafting is possible,
and the allowed states found along the valid branches.

The goal is to \strong{help fine-tune the grafting constraints before committing to a long run}:
the same checks are run at the start of \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} and \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}
(which stop if a taxon cannot be grafted), but here \strong{the problems are reported for all the taxa at once}, and the table can be explored.
}
\details{
\subsection{Taxa (\verb{$Target_clade} list)}{

For each taxon, the pool of branches available for grafting is built from its target clade, and restricted by its excluded subclades (see \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}}).
With a \code{simmap}, the pool is then restricted to the pieces of the branches in the allowed states of the taxon.
}

\subsection{Missing clades (\verb{$Sister_clade} list)}{

\strong{Taxa with the same sister clade are grafted together as one missing clade.}
The missing clade is grafted on the stem branch of its sister clade (see \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}}).
With a \code{simmap} the share of the stem branch in the allowed states is both the valid fraction and the share of the time window available.
The taxa of a missing clade must share their allowed states.
Results of the evaluation are reported with one row per missing clade.
}

\subsection{Enclosing clade}{

The enclosing clade is the target clade (or sister clade), or the smallest clade enclosing it with at least \code{min_tips} tips, in the backbone.
With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, it is used to fit the Birth-Death model and estimate divergence ages of the missing taxa to graft in accordance to the fitted diversification process.
It is reported so users can tune \code{min_tips} accordingly to define more relevant enclosing clades.
During the grafting, taxa already grafted in a clade count in its number of tips. The chosen enclosing clade used can therefore be the reported clade or a clade nested within it.
It can contain more tips than the one reported here, although the reported clade is the oldest one that can be used, and the number of tips reported is its number of backbone tips.

\strong{The sampling fractions of the birth-death models are not reported}: they depend on the taxa already grafted in the clade, so they cannot be known before grafting.
Use \code{\link[=extract_BD_model_summary]{phylograftR::extract_BD_model_summary()}} to extract \strong{a posteriori} the effective sampling fraction and diversification rates used during the grafting process.
}

\subsection{Several maps}{

With a \code{"multiSimmap"}, the plan is checked for each map, as attribute-based constraints are different across maps, and the table summarizes values across all maps
(i.e., minimum, mean and maximum of the valid fraction and of the time coverage, and the number of maps in which the taxon cannot be grafted).
The table per map is stored in the attribute \strong{\code{"per_map"}}.
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny and associated list of missing taxa
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

missing_taxa_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3"),
  Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5"))

# --- Check the grafting plan for taxonomic constraints --- #

# Check the grafting plan a priori to detect any issue at low cost
plan <- check_grafting_plan(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list, min_tips = 5)
plan

# --- Check the grafting plan for attribute-based constraints too --- #

# Add a simmap to restrict grafting to some states
Q <- matrix(0.04, 3, 3, dimnames = list(c("A", "B", "C"), c("A", "B", "C")))
diag(Q) <- -0.08
simmap <- phytools::sim.history(phy, Q, anc = "A", message = FALSE)

# Check the grafting plan a priori
plan_states <- check_grafting_plan(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                   simmap = simmap, allowed_states = "B")
# Inspect availability of branches in the allowed states
plan_states[, c("Taxon_name", "states_available", "valid_fraction", "time_coverage", "feasible")]


}
